#X text 174 352 For frequencies below 50 we actually have radians which work well and better for very very low frequencies near zero., f 27;
#X obj 98 600 lop2~;
#X obj 130 531 r \$0-hz;
#X obj 98 658 out~;
#X text 28 23 The [lop2~] is actually the exact same one pole filter as [lop~] with the same filter formula. The difference is in the frequency conversion to the filter coefficient formula \, which is more accurate and wroks well for higher frequencies. Another difference is on how the equation is implemented. It is different but it is mathematically equivalent., f 52;
#X text 221 222 This is a more accurate formula from the input in radians. It works for frequencies above 7 khz., f 24;
#X obj 686 152 r \$0-coeff;
#X text 469 68 This is the filter formula from the last example \, which is: y[n] = a*x[n] + b*y[-1], f 49;
#X obj 513 380 noise~;
#X obj 513 448 out~;
#X obj 738 416 r \$0-coeff;
#X text 485 297 Under the hood in [lop2~] we actually have a different formula that is mathematically equivalent. This the filter equation is then: y[n] = y[n-1] + k * (x[n] - y[n-1])., f 44;
#X obj 738 451 v k;
#X obj 513 412 fexpr~ $y + k * ($x - $y);
#X obj 572 659 out~;
#X text 510 528 Note that [lop2~] can also directly take the coefficient parameter., f 44;
#X obj 653 589 r \$0-coeff;
#X obj 572 585 noise~;
#X obj 572 620 lop2~ -coeff;
